      {
        "review_id": 183450481,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r183450481-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Unforgetable Experience",
        "text": "Pashupatinath is the oldest and one of the most important Hindu temples in Nepal and is listed in UNESCO World Heritage sites list. The Temple's existence dates back to 400 A.D, although the present temple was built in the 17th century after the previous one was consumed by termites. We walked along the 500m or so promenade to the temple proper, a walkway lined with little stalls selling incense, marigolds, beads and other religious bits and pieces. The Pashupati complex is huge containing many other old and important temples and shrines and is a popular spot for tourists and devotees. We visited on a weekday so there wasn’t a big crowd there. I found it a very moving experience watching cremations take place on the opposite side of the Bagmati river from where I was standing. But it’s all done in a business-like way with little fuss in accordance with the Hindu beliefs of the continuing cycle of birth, life, death and rebirth. For me it’s an unforgetable experience, one I definitely recommend if you’re coming to Nepal.",
